[metal] Hold command buffer commits while the app is in the background

iOS and tvOS refuse GPU work from an application in the background, and
Dawn 157 turns the refused command buffer (MTLCommandBufferErrorNotPermitted)
into a device loss (wcandillon/react-native-skia#4290). Dropping the work
instead is not safe either: Dawn marks the resources it writes initialized
while encoding, and a one-shot upload refused in the background (a glyph
atlas page, an image) would stay garbage for the session.

This patch holds the work instead, behind a device toggle,
metal_suspend_submissions_in_background. While the application is in the
background, submitted command buffers are encoded into the pending Metal
command buffer but not committed; presents are dropped. On entering the
background, the pending work is committed first and waited on to be
scheduled, which is what Apple asks before applicationDidEnterBackground
returns; on returning to the foreground, the held work is committed as one
command buffer. Nothing is lost or reordered: Dawn's bookkeeping, Skia
Graphite's completion tracking and the application's promises all describe
work that does run, later. A warning names a producer that keeps
submitting in the background, since each held submission pins what it
uses until the return.

The mechanism (SetSubmissionSuspended / IsSubmissionSuspended in
MetalBackend.h) is independent of the toggle, which only wires it to
UIApplication's notifications, and is what the white-box tests exercise.

Proposed upstream as the non-blocking form of the sleep suggested in
https://dawn-review.googlesource.com/c/dawn/+/352435. Drop this patch once
Dawn carries an equivalent.

---

